<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saqqez</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saqqiz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saqqez is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> province in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>. The area is rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> remains: the nearby site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziwiye">Ziwiye</a> produced a famous hoard of gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> objects of the 7th century BCE, and some scholars place the Mannaean capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izirtu">Izirtu</a> in its neighbourhood.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saqqez.jpg" alt="Saqqez" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caravaneternity · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saka2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Saqqez is the capital of both the Central District and the county of Saqqez in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> province. The city stands at an altitude of 1,476 metres, on long plains and hills among the irregular heights of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>, and the Zarrineh and Simineh rivers and their tributaries cross the area. The mountain setting gives it relatively cold weather and long winters, with hot, very dry summers and cold, snowy winters. Most of its people are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> who speak the Sorani dialect of Kurdish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Saqqez's history goes back to the seventh millennium BCE. Its name is said to derive from the Scythian Eskit and then Sakez; some sources link it instead with the Median ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyaxares">Cyaxares</a>, while other historians derive it from Sakez and attribute it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> who settled there in his reign. Finds such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziwiye">Ziwiye hoard</a> in Ziwiyeh Castle led experts such as Roman Ghirshman to place the ancient capital of the Median kingdom on the site of the modern city, and some place <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izirtu">Izirtu</a>, the capital of the Mannaeans, here.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-minorsky">Vladimir Minorsky</a>, Saqqez is the site of the medieval city of Barza. Theophanes the Confessor, who calls it Barzan, records that the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> stayed there for seven days in March 628 on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganzak">Ganzak</a>. Barza was an important crossroads in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>, where the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinavar">Dinavar</a> to Maragheh forked towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a>, and in the early 9th century it was the capital of a separate principality. David D'Beth Hillel, who died in 1846, stated that the city had a small Jewish community with one synagogue dating from around 1827.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2016 counted 165,258 people in the city. Saqqez has long been a place of culture and the arts, and each year at the end of autumn it holds a Kurdish Theater Festival at which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> groups from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> perform. Musicians and singers perform in the Kurdish language, and the city counts several well-known poets among its people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saqqez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the late 8th century they had become a unified kingdom with its capital at Izirtu (Zirta), perhaps near <b>Saqqez</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mannaea">Mannaea</a></li></ul></section>
